      "source": [
        "### Data Description & Methodology\n",
        "\n",
        "**Dataset Source:**\n",
        "\n",
        "This project uses the Formula 1 World Championship (1950–2024) dataset from Kaggle (rohanrao). It contains multiple tables with information on races,\n",
        "drivers, results, lap times, and other race details.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "**Time Range Used:**\n",
        "\n",
        "Even though the dataset goes back to 1950, this analysis focuses only on recent Formula 1 seasons (2021–2024). This is because modern F1 has more consistent rules, cars, and formats, so the results are more comparable and relevant to today’s racing.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Key Variables Used:**\n",
        "\n",
        "* raceId: Identifies each individual race\n",
        "* driverId: Identifies each driver\n",
        "* grid: Starting position from qualifying\n",
        "* position: Final finishing position in the race\n",
        "* position_change: Calculated as position - grid, showing how many places a driver gained or lost\n",
        "* forename, surname: Used to label and identify drivers in graphs\n",
        "\n",
        "**Data Cleaning & Preparation:**\n",
        "\n",
        "* The main datasets (races.csv, results.csv, drivers.csv, lap_times.csv) were loaded into pandas.\n",
        "* race_result was merged with races_df using raceId so each result is linked to its race information.\n",
        "* The grid and position columns were cleaned and converted into numeric values using pd.to_numeric(errors='coerce'), since some entries were not in a usable format.\n",
        "* Any missing values (like DNFs or incomplete results) were removed when calculating averages so they didn’t distort the results.\n",
        "* The dataset was filtered down to only include races from 2021–2024 for the main analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Data Aggregation:**\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead of looking at individual races, the data was grouped by driver so we could see overall patterns. For each driver, the average starting position and average finishing position were calculated across each season. From this, we calculated an average position change.\n",
        "\n",
        "This makes it easier to compare drivers fairly, since a single race (like a crash or mechanical failure) doesn’t completely distort the results.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why This Approach Was Used:**\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal was to understand whether qualifying position actually predicts race results, or if drivers regularly change their outcome during the race. Using season averages helps show the bigger pattern instead of focusing on one-off race results. Limiting the dataset to recent seasons also makes the analysis more relevant to modern Formula 1, where strategy and performance differences are more consistent."
      ]
    },
